Use `AbortSignal.timeout()`:

```js
const res = await fetch(url, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000) });
```

If the request takes longer than 5 seconds, the promise rejects with a `TimeoutError`:

```js
try {
  const res = await fetch(url, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(5000) });
  const data = await res.json();
} catch (err) {
  if (err.name === 'TimeoutError') {
    // request took longer than 5s
  } else {
    throw err;
  }
}
```

The timeout covers the whole operation that uses the signal, so a slow body download can also trigger it during `res.json()`, which is why that call sits inside the `try`.

`AbortSignal.timeout()` is supported in Node 17.3+ and all current browsers. For older environments, or if you also need to cancel manually, use an `AbortController`:

```js
const controller = new AbortController();
const timer = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 5000);
try {
  const res = await fetch(url, { signal: controller.signal });
  // read the body here so the timeout still applies to it
} finally {
  clearTimeout(timer);
}
```

In this version a timeout rejects with an `AbortError` rather than a `TimeoutError`.
